{"id":"bb514481d071","html":"<div><div><div><h2>The power of group thought</h2><p title=\"Duration\">16 MINUTES</p></div><p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/TKf3ZNrQ8S8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://www.youtube.com/embed/TKf3ZNrQ8S8</a></p><div><p>Dora Biro, a behavioural biologist and professor at the University of Rochester in New York, studies how homing pigeons learn from one another. By fitting them with GPS trackers, introducing new variables into flocks and observing how they respond, Biro has seen how pigeons’ flight routes improve in environments where they can share knowledge. In this talk from the Intelligence Everywhere festival held at Santa Monica College in <span>October 2026,</span> Biro discusses how these fascinating experiments were conducted, as well as what they tell us about the way in which collective intelligence can best be cultivated in human realms.</p></div><div><p>Explore more</p></div><div><p><img alt=\"People sitting around a table reading books with a tablet and mugs visible.\" width=\"2457\" height=\"1638\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/95e97620-7381-41d5-b4c1-26bd96535699/v2-final-gettyimages-1389876555.jpg?top=0&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=2457&amp;cropHeight=1638&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_35aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_35aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/guides/how-to-cultivate-intellectual-humility-for-better-group-thinking\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>How to cultivate collective intellectual humility</h4><p>Being smart is knowing what you don’t know. From work meetings to book clubs, use these tips to boost your group’s thinking</p><p>by Elizabeth Krumrei-Mancuso &amp; Mark Alfano</p></div><p><img alt=\"Black and white drawing of three men in hats sitting on a bench, each with distinct expressions and postures.\" width=\"1953\" height=\"1302\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/26bbdcc7-ec4d-4b2f-88c8-2a4e150d59b5/sz-96_68_91_cropped.jpg?top=0&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=1953&amp;cropHeight=1302&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_55aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_55aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/iq-scores-are-falling-but-no-were-not-growing-more-stupid\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>IQ scores are falling but, no, we’re not growing more stupid</h4><p>Screens and social media get the blame, but the real problem lies in how we measure intelligence in the first place</p><p>by Stuart Jeffries</p></div><div><p><img alt=\"Collage of animal photos with progressively simplified line drawings of a flamingo, giraffe and cat.\" width=\"2160\" height=\"1440\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/c274221e-0901-4737-8f25-61b402692ffd/making-the-invisible-visible-landscape-1.jpg?top=0&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=2160&amp;cropHeight=1440&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p></div><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_75aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_75aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/videos/from-cave-art-to-subatomic-sketches-how-drawing-has-accelerated-human-progress\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>From cave art to subatomic sketches – how drawing has accelerated human progress</h4><p>Video by MIT Quest for Intelligence</p></div><p><img alt=\"Black and white photo of people in a bustling bar, a man lighting a cigarette at a table with drinks and snacks.\" width=\"1800\" height=\"1200\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/4f8cfdc2-93e4-47bc-ae1a-6fd7cb6700b1/sq-simenon-166575286.jpeg?top=300&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=1800&amp;cropHeight=1200&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_95aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_95aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/guides/how-to-solve-problems-by-thinking-like-a-detective\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>How to think like a detective</h4><p>The best detectives seem to have almost supernatural insight, but their cognitive toolkit is one that anybody can use</p><p>by Ivar Fahsing</p></div><p><img alt=\"Photo of an airport terminal with travellers, a large US flag and a statue reflected in glass, showing modern architecture.\" width=\"5472\" height=\"3648\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/408cec2e-27bf-4240-a08b-723013321d9b/gettyimages-1237380078.jpeg?top=0&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=5472&amp;cropHeight=3648&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_b5aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_b5aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/the-collective-memory-bias-that-flatters-our-homelands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>The collective memory bias that flatters our homelands</h4><p>A community’s vision of the past is often selective and biased, exaggerating its own contributions relative to others</p><p>by Jeremy K Yamashiro &amp; Henry L Roediger, III</p></div><p><img alt=\"Photo of a person in a yellow T-shirt with a large black letter Q standing among seated people at an indoor event.\" width=\"3402\" height=\"2268\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/a37bba38-0032-41e2-9d53-cd741605cdb6/final-gettyimages-1848354204.jpeg?top=0&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=3402&amp;cropHeight=2268&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_d5aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_d5aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/the-surprising-role-of-deep-thinking-in-conspiracy-theories\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>The surprising role of deep thinking in conspiracy theories</h4><p>People who endorse and spread outlandish theories aren’t gullible, they’re drawn to the intoxicating lure of discovery</p><p>by Stephen Gadsby &amp; Sander Van de Cruys</p></div><p><img alt=\"An otter rolling on a moss-covered rock, eyes closed in a relaxed pose with a blurry background.\" width=\"4992\" height=\"3328\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/eaef65c0-d62f-487b-9d37-15e554c679f5/caroline-legg-flickr-46498316465_d53e91b284_o.jpg?top=0&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=4992&amp;cropHeight=3328&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_f5aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_f5aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/ideas/you-can-think-like-an-animal-by-silencing-your-chattering-brain\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>You can think like an animal by silencing your chattering brain</h4><p>The desire is partly whimsical, but a brief shapeshift across the taxonomic gulf could help us better empathise with animals</p><p>by Bryony Tolhurst</p></div><p><img alt=\"Illustration of a woman on a bench by a cityscape with a red sun and blooming branches, conveying serenity and nature.\" width=\"1998\" height=\"1332\" data-nimg=\"1\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1440px) 360px, (min-width: 1024px) 25vw, 100vw\" src=\"https://images.aeonmedia.co/images/8be95d03-f772-472a-964d-4e64a1aabba2/final-aeon-flat-sized.jpg?top=208&amp;left=0&amp;cropWidth=1998&amp;cropHeight=1332&amp;width=3840&amp;quality=75&amp;format=auto\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></p><a aria-labelledby=\"_R_h5aucnpfiv4tb_-title\" aria-describedby=\"_R_h5aucnpfiv4tb_-description\" data-ga-carousel=\"Explore more\" href=\"https://psyche.co/guides/how-to-cultivate-shoshin-or-a-beginners-mind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"></a><div><h4>How to foster ‘shoshin’</h4><p>It’s easy for the mind to become closed to new ideas. Cultivating a beginner’s mind helps us rediscover the joy of learning</p><p>by Christian Jarrett</p></div></div></div></div>"}